※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=7 diag=none

※ A documentation comment is prose, and the parts of one written the way Doxygen
※ writes them are about particular parts of what is documented.
※
※ **The commands are Doxygen's own, with either sigil.**  `\param`, `\return`,
※ `\brief` and the rest, written after a `\` or an `@` -- which is Doxygen's own
※ rule and means a comment moved here from C keeps working.  `@` is the
※ attribute sigil everywhere else in this language, and there is no ambiguity:
※ an attribute is never inside a comment.
※
※ **What the compiler does with them is check them.**  A `\param` names a
※ parameter, so whether the function has one of that name is a question with an
※ answer, and a comment that gets it wrong tells a reader about something that is
※ not there.  The four ways of being wrong are warnings, on unless turned off;
※ `doc-comment-wrong` is the program that raises them.
※
※ **What an editor does with them is show them.**  The summary first, then what
※ it takes as a list, then what it answers with -- which is what a hover over the
※ name shows, out of this comment and nothing else.
※
※ Compare: **Doxygen** itself, and **Javadoc**, whose `@param` this is with the
※ other sigil; **Rust**, whose `///` is Markdown with no commands at all and
※ whose parameters are therefore documented in prose; **Go**, the same; **D**,
※ whose Ddoc has named sections rather than commands.  What this takes from
※ Doxygen is the spelling a reader already knows, and what it leaves is
※ everything that is markup.

※※ Add two numbers, saturating rather than wrapping.
※※
※※ \details The sum of two numbers that will not fit in one is the largest
※※ number that does fit, which is what saturating means.
※※ \param left the number on the left
※※ \param right the number on the right
※※ \return the sum, or the largest u8 where it does not fit
@[visible]
fn total(left: u8, right: u8) → u8:
    left ⊞ right

※※ \brief What a program answers with, where the whole comment is one line.
※※ @param a the first
※※ @param b the second
@[visible, impure]
fn remember(a: u8, b: u8):
    kept ← total(a, b)

let kept: mut u8 = 0u8

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    remember(3u8, 4u8)
    ⎕narrow(kept, ⌜u6⌝) ?? 1u6
